::STAT_U01_UA_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey records favorite school subject. What type of data is collected?
{
=A. Categorical#Correct. Favorite subject is a label or group, not a measured amount.
~B. Numerical#No numerical quantity is measured.
~C. Bivariate numerical#Only one categorical variable is recorded.
~D. Neither#Category responses are valid data.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records study time and quiz score for each student. Which display best fits?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart is for one categorical variable.
=B. Scatterplot#Correct. The data contain two paired numerical variables.
~C. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
~D. Bar chart#A bar chart compares category counts.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram interval 31-45 minutes has height 10. What does the height mean?
{
~A. The average is 10 minutes#The height is not a mean.
~B. The interval is 10 minutes wide#The endpoints define interval width.
=C. Ten values fall in that interval#Correct. Histogram height represents frequency for the interval.
~D. The data are categorical#Histogram intervals are numerical.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table gives bus as 0.40. What percent of the segmented bar should bus occupy?
{
~A. 4%#This conversion is too small.
~B. 60%#This is the complement, not the listed value.
~C. 400%#This conversion is too large.
=D. 40%#Correct. 0.40 equals 40%.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display fits grade level and lunch choice?
{
=A. Two-way table#Correct. Both variables are categorical.
~B. Histogram#A histogram shows one numerical variable.
~C. Scatterplot#A scatterplot uses two numerical variables.
~D. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical data.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A scatterplot shows points rising from left to right. What conclusion is reasonable?
{
~A. One variable definitely caused the other#Association does not prove causation.
=B. The variables have a positive association#Correct. An upward pattern supports positive association.
~C. The variables are categorical#Scatterplots use numerical variables.
~D. There is no relationship#The upward pattern suggests a relationship.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Why compare relative frequencies when group totals are different?
{
~A. They remove categories#Categories remain in the comparison.
~B. They prove cause#Relative frequencies describe data but do not prove cause.
=C. Relative frequencies compare proportions fairly#Correct. They account for unequal group sizes.
~D. They make raw counts equal#They do not change original counts.
}

::STAT_U01_UA_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
What should be checked before interpreting a data display?
{
~A. Only color#Color alone does not define meaning.
~B. Only the largest mark#The largest mark needs context.
~C. Only the file name#The file name is not the data display.
=D. Title, labels, units, scale, and context#Correct. These components define what the quantities mean.
}

